plan RuntimeV2
title   Runtime ownership and actor lifecycle
status  archived
goal
    Realize the migrated vNext requirements in one managed runtime with closed application
    admission, explicit identity, ordered actor publication, and lifecycle-safe host
    integration. This Plan owns migrated semantics under @plan.Verification.D1; source
    contracts supply provenance and trace IDs. The rewrite is incomplete, so this Plan
    claims no implementation or product cutover; see @plan.Cutover.O3 and @plan.Cutover.V7.
outcomes
    O1  Definitions, machine behavior, input, and state
        Definition is the trusted in-process TypeScript boundary for authoring one machine.
        It includes explicit identity, named state/event schemas, input, provider selectors,
        a pure initial-memory factory, and a flat named operation catalogue. Definition
        describes one complete static actor family and contains no transition or activity
        behavior. A package-private Effect Schema synchronously decodes the
        ordinary authored object before nominal definition, state-token, event-token,
        event-envelope, and context-selector values are constructed. Authors use
        callbacks or null for events; they do not import or supply Schema values.

        Authored IDs and local state/event names retain exact spelling. Each is
        non-empty and at most 256 UTF-8 bytes; reject C0 controls, DEL, lone surrogates,
        and NUL before a value becomes definition-derived or durable identity. Static
        types preserve exact literals but do not claim UTF-8-byte validation. Do not
        normalize Unicode or use locale comparison. Composite IDs use a fixed namespace
        tag and length-prefixed UTF-8 segments of the form <utf8ByteLength>:<segment>;
        delimiter concatenation is not identity. Use host-neutral WHATWG UTF-8 via
        TextEncoder. Reject lone surrogates with String.prototype.isWellFormed when
        available or a small equivalent fallback. Keep Node Buffer outside the package
        contract. Supported hosts must produce identical validation and encoding. Stable
        actor refs use actor: followed by length-prefixed machine-ID and authored-ID
        segments; opaque refs are runtime-local with no durable form.
        State tokens preserve complete paths such as S.ACTIVE.S.EDITING; events use
        one machine-wide protocol.

        Accept only complete definition-derived tokens and stable-ref encoding. Reject
        invalid names, normalization-dependent identity, locale ordering, raw delimiter
        IDs, partial or duplicate declarations, excess top-level fields, and invalid
        declaration kinds. Reject event payloads that are arrays, have custom prototypes,
        or contain an authored type field.
        The authoring boundary makes no stable rejection or error-translation promise for
        proxies, throwing getters, descriptor tricks, or symbols. The same limit applies
        to sparse arrays, custom container prototypes, and mutation during construction.
        This boundary is not a security boundary against the caller.
        Equal source spellings are equal only by exact spelling; composed and decomposed
        spellings remain distinct.

        A machine is reusable behavior bound to one definition. One actor is one live
        instance with its own ref, mailbox, state, memory, context, operation ownership,
        publications, and lifecycle. Machine behavior owns transition legality,
        guards, memory updates, redirects, timers, activities, compound behavior, and
        context-to-event registrations. Recursive substates share the actor memory,
        context, event protocol, mailbox, operations, and lifetime; they have no actor,
        input, completion, snapshot, or address. Machine states are mutually exclusive
        durable modes; resource, transaction, stream, and timer statuses are overlapping
        operation lifecycles, not a Cartesian state product. Any number of independent
        actors may use one machine. Child actors, child inputs/completions/addresses,
        and operation statuses as machine states are excluded.

        Input is supplied once when a fresh actor is created. The sole source for
        InputOf and MemoryOf is memory: ({ input }) => Memory; omitting its argument
        fixes input to void. Memory is actor-local mutable domain data, changed only by
        accepted machine transitions; it is not cache, server projection, operation
        status, or an escape hatch. Context is a pure inherited projection from exact
        provider refs. Restored actors retain serialized memory and never replay input
        or invoke the initializer. Input never changes on a running actor and does not
        classify local versus shared actors.

        Ordered context turns may change context. When context identifies another domain
        instance, the host replaces or re-keys the actor. It does not retain memory across
        that identity change.

        Definition construction decodes static shape once and returns Result.
        Machine construction consumes that typed success value, tokens, selectors,
        operations,
        and retained initializer. It does not import the private Schema, re-decode
        configuration, or repeat definition validation. A fresh actor invokes the pure
        memory factory exactly once with input, or materializes canonical empty memory.
        Root and entered compound defaults resolve to one exact leaf; redirects stabilize
        outermost-to-innermost without intermediate publication or work. Restore installs
        materialized memory without initialization or input replay. Machine behavior
        thereafter receives memory, context, state, and events, not original input.
        Initializer/selector/redirect defects abort admission without partial actor
        escape.

        State declarations are recursive, exact, and at most ten levels deep. A leaf is
        a string; a compound is a single-key object with an ordered state list. Root and
        compound configuration requires default and exact nested states. Public state is one
        definition-derived active-leaf token. `matches` accepts that
        leaf or an active ancestor. `reenter` names an exact active boundary. It releases
        that boundary's active path and re-enters its target inside it. Inactive, foreign,
        or non-containing boundaries reject. Redirects stabilize outermost-to-leaf and preserve
        authored order per node. Terminal-looking leaves remain ordinary leaves: no final kind,
        parent completion, final output, auto-disposal, stream completion, or mailbox
        shutdown. Compound activities/timers own active lifetimes. AppPlan cannot grow
        after construction.
    O2  Closed application graph and durable identity
        A module is inert tooling metadata with a unique app-local ID and exact keyed
        machines. An app has an explicit ID, persistence version, ordered unaliased
        modules, and AppPlan. Flattening preserves machine property names. Duplicate
        durable machine IDs, machine values, or tooling owners fail even where property
        names differ. App IDs are explicit and collision-free. A machine's explicit id
        is durable machine/artifact identity; an App.M property is only an ergonomic
        TypeScript key. Module IDs group CLI slicing, traces, inspection, behavior
        artifacts, and module diffs, but are not machine, actor, persistence, context,
        runtime, or operation identity. Renaming/moving tooling ownership is
        artifact-breaking: use a different group/path and no silent compare/alias;
        runtime/persistence identity stays compatible and history needs migration.
        Local, shared, and Story-local creation may use only machines listed in App.M.

        Operation descriptors are inert reusable configuration for one named resource,
        transaction, or stream family. Definitions admit them through flat named
        operations; machines receive exact actor-bound families through O. Descriptor
        ID names a family, never parameterized work. Declaring/reading a descriptor,
        projecting a key, or constructing an unreturned plan starts no work. The named
        catalogue is closed: descriptor-specific key, passive getData/getState,
        resource lookup/subscribe/refetch/setData/actor-owned cancel, transaction
        commit/cancel, and stream subscribe only. No generic registry, operation
        reference, bound entry, or public enumeration.

        P is complete executable input retained by a live lookup, transaction attempt,
        or stream subscription; it may contain clients/functions and is never identity.
        K is the synchronously projected ordered canonical tuple. Resource/transaction
        identity is descriptor ID plus K; a live stream also retains its declaration
        slot. Service dependencies belong to inferred R; runtime-owned capabilities
        stay outside P. Canonicalization accepts ordinary dense arrays and plain records,
        then copies them into Flow-owned containers. It sorts record keys and normalizes
        -0 to 0. It rejects hostile reflection and unsupported values, then uses the
        bounded UTF-8 KBytes grammar. Capability, tenant, account, network, permission,
        session, and every
        result-changing discriminator must be in K; runtime partitioning is not a
        substitute. Do not treat P as identity, reconstruct P from K, add a second
        identity projection/custom equality/hash, or omit result-changing discriminators.
        Equal canonical keys mean equivalent canonical results only within one runtime
        identity domain. Exact identity laws are owned once by @O2 and are referenced by
        @plan.OperationsV2.O1, @plan.OperationsV2.O2,
        @plan.OperationsV2.O3, and @plan.OperationsV2.O4.

        Keep machine, actor, operation, Story, and recipe identities separate:

        - Each actor has exact `ActorRef<M>`. Stable refs are durable and shared; opaque
        refs are local to one Runtime.
        - Operation identity is descriptor ID plus K. P need not be reconstructible.
        - A Story recipe uses object identity within one run. It carries exact machine,
        fresh input, and compatible bindings, but no Runtime or live owner.
        - `RunLocalId` identifies a Story run/recipe. `ActorIncarnationId` identifies one
        Runtime lifetime; one recipe may create multiple incarnations.
        - Descriptor IDs follow definition-name grammar and validate during pure app
        compilation.

        Proposed module/app example from CONTRACT.md §4 (provenance only; not a new API
        commitment):
        ```ts
        const CoreModule = module({ id: "core", machines: { router: routerMachine, auth: authMachine } });
        const TodoApp = app({
          id: "todo-app",
          persistenceVersion: "1",
          modules: [CoreModule, TodosModule],
        });
        TodoApp.M.router;
        ```
    O3  Runtime construction, readiness, and admission
        Readiness is private state exposed only by runtime.ready(). It starts bootstrap
        at most once and caches its terminal Exit. No real handle, lease, or command
        escapes before graph seal and readiness; the sole exception is an inert prepared
        React handle. The phase table lists constructed, booting, ready, failed, and
        disposed. Do not publish a phase
        union or second readiness API. Failure closes admission; disposal during
        construction/bootstrap wins, cancels bootstrap, reverses acquired ownership,
        and terminalizes. Private readiness state never becomes a machine event.

        Phase boundary table for the private runtime lifecycle:

        | Phase | Admission | Required boundary |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | constructed | none | service-free shell only |
        | booting | bootstrap/activation | prepare, Implementation, install, ensure, seal |
        | ready | ordinary work | all attached actors crossed activation barrier |
        | failed | closed | cleanup only |
        | disposed | closed | terminal |

        `runtime.ready()` follows the selected restore order:

        1. Read, decode, and validate v2 records; prepare detached resource, overlay,
           and actor data. Do not install StoreKernel state, initialize or register
           actors, or restore operations.
        2. Acquire Implementation services.
        3. Install prepared store state, declared persistable actors, and operations;
           restore provider actors before consumers.
        4. Complete initial ensures and resolve exact context bindings.
        5. Seal the graph, then activate actors.
        6. Expose handles only after activation.

        Mutable post-start hydration is forbidden. A capture cut is context-closed, between
        completed propagation waves, and retains exact binding refs/provider revisions.
        Reject missing, foreign, duplicate, or cyclic providers before activation. Reject
        partial graph escape and opaque local provider promotion, recreation, substitution,
        or rebinding. Reject capture mismatch. ConcurrentDehydrate is retryable;
        NonDurableContextProvider is not.

        runtimeSetup names an app and complete Implementation when inferred requirements
        are not never. Resource, transaction, stream, machine, module, and app types keep
        the complete inferred environment. Runtime removes only Flow-owned Scope.Scope;
        descriptor R remains raw. Omitted Implementation is valid only for never
        requirements. Implementation acquisition failure is readiness failure, never
        an operation outcome; services are installed only at the runtime boundary.
        Definition/machine compilation exposes Schema-backed Diagnostic through Result;
        expected failures use no feature-owned Error class or Data.TaggedError carrier.

        Runtime construction example:
        ```ts
        import { Effect } from "effect";
        
        const setup = flow.runtimeSetup({
          app: TodoApp,
          implementation: TodoLive,
          persistence: persistence({ storage, scope: "user:42" }),
        });
        
        const runtime = setup.construct();
        await Effect.runPromise(runtime.ready());
        const lease = runtime.ensureActor(TodoRefs.primary, {
          input: { userId: "user-42" },
          contextBindings,
        });
        lease.actor.send(Todo.E.RefreshRequested());
        ```
        RuntimeSetup discovery is synchronous/inert; construct returns the runtime handle.
        Detached preparation, Implementation acquisition, prepared-state installation,
        initial admission, graph sealing, and activation occur at ready. ready is the
        only public readiness Effect.
        Only a Runtime bridge or final host adapter executes it. Host ingress and bridge
        ownership are detailed in @plan.Hosts.O2. Internal services and
        workflows return Effects with Diagnostic as expected failure and preserve Cause
        for defects/interruption. Convert each foreign Promise API once in a named
        Effect.tryPromise({ try, catch }) adapter; do not nest conversion or runners.

        Stable actor refs are inert and machine-branded. `actorRef(machine, id,
        { persist?: boolean })` contains identity only. Its wire form is `actor:` plus
        length-prefixed UTF-8 machine-ID and authored stable-ID segments.

        - `ensureActor` restores or creates one shared actor and returns `{ actor, dispose }`.
        - Concurrent ensures join one construction.
        - `getActor` is lookup-only; it never creates, adopts, leases, or grants disposal.
        - `createActor` always makes a fresh local actor with an opaque ref and no stable ID.
        - A ref is branded to its exact machine, not one app. Runtime app identity
          qualifies persisted actor identity.
        - Each stable ref per runtime incarnation has one shared lease and lifetime.
          There are no per-caller refcounts.
        - Boot-restored actors receive the runtime lease before handles escape.
        - If Runtime does not ensure a stable actor, runtime ownership lasts until explicit
          disposal or runtime shutdown.
        - Stable disposal tombstones that ref until runtime shutdown. Another runtime may
          reuse it.

        Exact context bindings map each declared slot to one provider ref. Missing,
        ambiguous, foreign, mismatched, disposed, tombstoned, cyclic, or opaque-provider
        cases fail before side effects. Admission validates the complete binding graph
        atomically and rolls back in reverse order.

        Dehydration includes every registered, non-disposed, durable stable actor,
        including suspended and boot-restored actors. It also includes their transitive
        stable-provider closure. It excludes local, disposed, and tombstoned actors.
        Opaque providers fail closed.

        Post-bootstrap `ensureActor` and `createActor` follow this admission sequence:

        1. Validate app/plan provenance, machine/ref identity, input, bindings, providers,
           tombstones, duplicate instance identity, and cycles before registry or
           StoreState mutation.
        2. Install a silent context baseline and logical edges. Attach and activate before
           exposing the lease.

        Concurrent ensures for one stable identity join one terminal owner admission.
        Failure before lease visibility reverses staged work, edges, mailbox, registration,
        and prepared actor. Failure after visibility uses production disposal and retains
        cleanup truth. No partial handle, snapshot, generation, or evidence may escape.

        Persistence provider configuration and codec/storage ownership belong to
        @plan.Artifacts.O1. Runtime sequences that provider with actor restoration.
        Persistence is one inert RuntimeSetup host provider. It owns storage, codec,
        restoration, observation, ordered writes, and cleanup. `persist` is declaration
        metadata on stable refs or named resource, transaction, and stream descriptors.
        It defaults to false and is not identity.

        Persistable actors capture complete stable snapshots. Operations contribute only
        their defined family facts. Persistence does not choose the durable app set. An
        identity-only filter may exclude declared entries but cannot select undeclared
        entries. The codec encodes and decodes application values. Its default accepts
        bounded JSON-safe values and rejects unsupported values without silent coercion.
        Persistence is not part of RequirementsOf<App>.
        Runtime readiness validates providers, codecs, AppPlan, refs, input, bindings,
        requirements, tombstones, cycles, persisted identity/versions/bounds, and context
        closure before activation/handle escape.

        CONTRACT.md §§4–5 are unaccepted proposal provenance and do not override this Plan.
        Its module/app example is retained under @O2. Its actor/lease usage is future
        target only; the proposal says its temporary shell has only app, construct(),
        and ready(). The proposal's temporary `ready` lazily acquires complete
        Implementation providers
        sequentially once per shell. Each shell caches its first terminal Exit exactly
        once: success, typed ImplementationError failure, defect, or interruption. The
        proposal preserves typed failure, defect, and interruption in Cause. That
        shell has no persistence, actors, Effect bridges, lifecycle, disposal, or
        Diagnostic mapping. This is proposal provenance, not a claim about current code
        or an accepted replacement for this Plan's requirements. Keep the complete future target
        separate. Readiness maps provider, storage, codec, and restoration failures to
        Effect<void, Diagnostic>. It includes Runtime bridges, actor methods, and
        asynchronous disposal.

        CONTRACT.md §4 future-target actor/lease illustration (proposal provenance):
        ```ts
        const ref = actorRef(editorMachine, "primary-editor", { persist: true });
        const sharedLease = runtime.ensureActor(ref, { input, contextBindings });
        const sharedActor = runtime.getActor(ref);
        const localLease = runtime.createActor(editorMachine, { input, contextBindings });
        await sharedLease.dispose();
        ```

        The ordinary actor handle exposes only `actor.ref`, `actor.send(event): void`,
        `getSnapshot()`, and `actor.snapshots`. It grants no disposal, ownership, mailbox,
        receipt, raw Cause, or operation-registry authority.

        `send` synchronously admits the exact typed event to the production mailbox and
        returns void. It yields no Promise, Effect, Fiber, snapshot, actor, acknowledgment,
        or subscription. A synchronous admission error is reported before work. Only an
        owner lease or whole-runtime disposal owns actor disposal.

        CONTRACT.md §5 proposed Implementation illustration and surface:
        ```ts
        const TodoLive = Implementation.succeed(
          TodoGateway,
          TodoGateway.of({
            list: ({ listId }, { signal }) => fetchTodos(listId, { signal }),
            add: ({ listId, title }, { signal }) => createTodo(listId, title, { signal }),
          }),
        );
        const TodoStory = Implementation.succeed(
          TodoGateway,
          TodoGateway.of({
            list: () => Effect.succeed(initialTodos),
            add: ({ title }) => Effect.succeed({ id: "todo-1", title, completed: false }),
          }),
        );
        const TodoWithClock = Implementation.merge(TodoLive, ClockLive);
        ```
        The proposal allows exactly three namespace methods: `succeed`, `effect`, and
        `merge`.

        - `succeed` is a complete synchronous, non-failing provider.
        - `effect` acquires at most once per Runtime. Its typed error is an implementation
          readiness failure. Runtime owns its scope and finalizer.
        - `merge` composes providers without changing service identity.

        Complete generic signatures for `effect` and `merge` remain unpublished. The
        proposal allows no further method. Duplicate service identity rejects, and order
        never selects a winner. These details remain proposal provenance unless an
        owning Plan confirms them.

        CONTRACT.md §5 proposed runtimeSetup signatures:
        ```ts
        function runtimeSetup<A extends App>(options: {
          app: A;
          // Deferred until the Persistence owner lands; retained for restoration.
          // persistence?: Persistence;
        }): RuntimeSetup<A, never>;
        function runtimeSetup<A extends App, ImplementationError>(options: {
          app: A;
          implementation: Implementation<RequirementsOf<A>, ImplementationError>;
          // Deferred until the Persistence owner lands; retained for restoration.
          // persistence?: Persistence;
        }): RuntimeSetup<A, ImplementationError>;
        ```
        Proposed accepted-call examples:
        ```ts
        runtimeSetup({ app: PublicApp });
        runtimeSetup({ app: ProjectApp, implementation: ProjectLive });
        ```
        The proposal limits the first overload to never requirements and otherwise
        closes Implementation over RequirementsOf<App>. Its temporary ready error is
        ImplementationError, not Diagnostic. Do not equate that proposal with the
        normative future target.

        CONTRACT.md §5 proposed future complete-runtime usage:
        ```ts
        const setup = flow.runtimeSetup({
          app: TodoApp,
          implementation: TodoLive,
          persistence: persistence({ storage, scope: "user:42" }),
        });
        
        const runtime = setup.construct();
        await Effect.runPromise(runtime.ready());
        const lease = runtime.ensureActor(TodoRefs.primary, {
          input: { userId: "user-42" },
          contextBindings,
        });
        lease.actor.send(Todo.E.RefreshRequested());
        ```
        Proposed future readiness validates all inputs before activation or handle escape:

        - Validate providers, codecs, AppPlan, refs, input, bindings, requirements,
          tombstones, and cycles.
        - Validate persisted identity, versions, bounds, and context closure.
        - Restore providers before consumers, install derived context silently, and seal
          the graph.
        - Activate actors before exposing handles.

        Stories, React, SSR, requests, live hosts, and CLI use these same owners. Private
        phases, Effect runtime composition, cells, queues, and shell layout are not
        compatibility surfaces when these conditions hold:

        - Construction stays inert.
        - Readiness starts at most once and caches terminal Exit.
        - No usable handle escapes early.
        - Failure, interruption, and disposal release each acquired owner exactly once,
          without partial graph or evidence.
    O4  Actor turns, publication, and failure truth
        Each accepted event/timer macrostep follows this order:

        1. Select one transition and evaluate its guard.
        2. Evaluate `updateMemory`, then actions. Apply target and memory, then stabilize
           redirects.
        3. Materialize and validate the complete finite-action batch. Stage actor/store
           changes.
        4. Commit and publish, then start or join asynchronous work.

        The first authored eligible transition wins. No winning transition is a no-op
        without issue or transition. `updateMemory` shallow-merges once against pre-turn
        memory; redirects do not rerun it.

        Redirects read candidate state, memory, and snapshot, never the original event.
        They preserve authored transition order and re-enter only their declared boundary.
        Compound redirects remain applicable while a descendant is active, including
        after descendant transitions or memory updates. Active-path compound redirects run
        outermost to leaf, preserving authored order within each node.

        `can(event)` performs the same pure eligibility query. It does not mutate, publish
        issues, redirect, materialize actions, or admit operations.
        First-declined guards stop later callbacks. Candidate reads are unavailable to
        actions. A complete invalid batch leaves no valid prefix. Microstep 101, duplicate
        ancestor/descendant handlers, and timer-inferred finite actions reject. Planning
        defects discard the whole candidate with no state, memory, activity, occurrence,
        store, or external-work residue.

        Timer guards/updates receive state, memory, snapshot, and timer { name,
        startedAt, dueAt }. Timers target explicit events only; an after timer may target
        an explicit refresh event, never finite actions directly. Timer-owned actions
        are not inferred. The timer turn and its explicit target event are separate FIFO
        macrosteps, each with its own pre-turn memory and one optional shallow-merged update.
        Append the target event after timer publication; already queued work may intervene.
        Accepted actions materialize finite plans only after guard
        acceptance; onMemory reconciles continuing plans separately. User Effects never
        run during planning or before the actor snapshot. One private CommitPlan holds
        store changes, desired state-activity ownership, primitive facts, mapped-operation
        intentions, and issues. Commit reserves generations, applies atomic actor/store
        changes, stages work, then starts/stops Effects only from post-publication
        reconciliation queued on the same mailbox. Projection-only facts reread
        authoritative state and publish one full snapshot without transition evaluation;
        mapped events are later ordinary turns. No second mutable plan owner exists.

        | Stage | Reads | May publish/acquire |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | plan | one pre-turn snapshot + causal event | nothing |
        | candidate | target, updated memory, redirects | nothing until valid |
        | validate | complete finite-action batch | nothing on failure |
        | commit | staged actor/store facts | one atomic publication |
        | reconcile | committed plan | async work after publication |

        A successful turn exposes one atomic committed actor/store cut. No observer sees
        an initiating store revision without its corresponding committed actor snapshot.
        Accepted evidence corresponds only to the committed cut and is globally ordered.
        Acknowledgment follows actor/store publication and evidence acceptance but never
        waits for user Effects. StoreFanout preserves causal order. Permit/reservation/
        queue/gate choreography is private. Issue-only publication advances only actor
        publication revision; ordinary machine publication advances actor publication
        and machine-turn revisions. Sequence overflow after mutation, inline sinks,
        acknowledgment after user Effect settlement, disposal interrupting half a
        published turn, and stale/partial fanout are forbidden. Runtime owns actor
        publication; Store commit ownership and the capture barrier are in
        @plan.OperationsV2.O1.

        Public send(event): void admits synchronously and returns void; it does not await
        Implementation, a turn, or async work. It returns no Promise, Effect, Fiber,
        snapshot, actor, acknowledgment, or subscription. Internal/Story dispatch creates
        one private acknowledgment synchronously. For publishing turns it resolves after
        publication and TurnRecord acceptance, before sink processing or later work.
        A no-handler or all-guards-decline event completes its ordered eligibility decision
        as a silent no-op, with no publication, issue, TurnRecord, or associated revision.
        Its acknowledgment still settles once; unused reservations return without replay.
        Story send waits
        only for that acknowledgment; contained-defect acknowledgment follows its
        issue-only publication/TurnRecord. No direct transition invocation, unrelated
        ready-work draining, or acknowledgment for discarded candidate work.

        Each public snapshot comes from one published turn. It contains:

        - Exact active leaf state, memory, inherited context, lifecycle, and issues.
        - Safe-integer public revision and observed store revision.
        - Typed named-operation readers and resource, transaction, and stream projections.
        - Timer state.

        Callback state is the exact active leaf token. `matches` may recognize that leaf
        or an active ancestor. Every machine-turn snapshot is complete. Projection-only,
        lifecycle-only, and issue-only snapshots are also complete and follow their own
        revision effects. Private machine-turn revision is not a second public field.
        `getSnapshot` and snapshot streams expose one value per publication.

        Streams replay the latest truth to late subscribers. Prepared subscriptions replay
        prepared, then become live at activation. Suspended subscriptions replay suspended
        once and complete. Disposed subscriptions replay terminal state, then complete.
        Disposed streams emit through terminal state and complete. Lifecycle snapshot is
        published before lifecycle evidence. Do not expose receipts, diagnostics, pending
        outcomes, handled, errors, raw Cause, child
        projections, nested XState-style state, final-node kind, actor output, or a second
        public revision. Terminal-looking leaves do not complete actor, stream, or
        mailbox.

        Retain Exit and full Cause through classification with Effect.exit/onExit. Public
        diagnostic precedence is defect, typed failure, then interruption-only; empty or
        unclassifiable failure is invariant defect. Only FlowDisposeError and
        FlowStoryExecutionError preserve complete Cause.Cause<unknown>. Squash only at
        intentionally lossy JS throw/rejection boundaries. Do not use Effect.result as
        complete truth, Cause.squash internally, or expose raw Cause in actor snapshots.
        Runtime bridges and final host adapters are the only runner edges. Reusable
        workflows, services, or adapters do not call runPromise*, runSync*, or related
        runners. Effect.promise is forbidden. Map Promise rejections in a named
        Effect.tryPromise({ try, catch }) adapter.

        For a callback defect before commit, discard the candidate and retain prior
        state/memory/bindings/store revision. Publish one issue-only active snapshot and
        private ordered stable diagnostics; admit no occurrence/work/store change and do
        not retry. Invariant defect closes admission, fails current/buffered acknowledgments,
        finalizes work, and publishes disposed fatal/cleanup truth. After commit,
        publication uses non-failing primitives; abandoning it is invariant failure.
        Guard, redirect, timer, memory, binding, key, target, and selector failures share
        the prior-truth rule. Do not publish a partial candidate, retry issue-only work,
        or claim committed StoreState was rolled back because snapshot publication failed.
    O5  Actor lifecycle and context
        Lifecycle is exactly prepared | active | suspended | disposed. Only an explicit
        owner lease disposes an actor individually. Disposal is asynchronous, idempotent,
        and terminal. It closes admission before cleanup; Runtime disposal subsumes leases.
        Stable shared disposal leaves a Runtime-incarnation tombstone until Runtime
        disposal. Reject disposal while active or suspended consumers remain bound, and
        name dependent refs and keys.

        React cleanup is genuine suspension:

        - Preserve the ref, handle, state, memory, context, operation records, cursors, and
          absolute deadlines.
        - Close command admission at one linearization point.
        - Drain pre-close commands FIFO.
        - Detach registrations, subscriptions, activities, and timers.
        - Interrupt unsettled finite work. Close streams and retain cursors/pending truth.
        - Publish suspended only after cleanup.

        React attachment details belong to @plan.Hosts.O3. Resume waits for
        finalizers, reacquires continuing work, reinstalls
        absolute timers, performs at most one overdue refresh, and routes provider changes
        through one ordinary context wave. It never replays input, initialization,
        committed events, finite actions, or emissions. Resume after a cleanup defect is
        blocked until owner/runtime disposal. Suspended actors retain logical context
        edges and no live attachment resources. Lifecycle transitions:

        | From | Allowed transition | Meaning |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | prepared | active | attach/activate same prepared actor |
        | active | suspended | serialized attachment cleanup |
        | suspended | active | resume after cleanup/finalizers |
        | any non-disposed | disposed | owner/runtime terminal disposal |
        | disposed | none | terminal |

        CONTRACT.md §4's lifecycle/attachment table is proposal provenance only. It does
        not promote that proposed API surface over this Plan's requirements:

        | Lifecycle | Commands | Attachments |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | prepared | Buffer >=64; exact limit is implementation-defined | Live on activation |
        | active | Admit | Remain live |
        | suspended | Reject; do not buffer | Release attachments; retain continuity |
        | disposed | Reject | Terminal snapshot/replay; complete |

        A handle rejects after disposal. Stable tombstones reject getActor/ensureActor
        for that Runtime incarnation. Disposal is reverse-dependency ordered and blocked
        by active/suspended dependents with precise diagnostics.

        React preparation, attachment, command buffering, `useActor`, `useActorByRef`,
        `useView`, selector equality, and MachineObserver behavior belong to
        @plan.Hosts.O3. Runtime supplies the prepared actor lifecycle and publications
        consumed by that host boundary. Operation-reader behavior belongs to
        @plan.OperationsV2.O4.

        Issue is current structured failure/defect/interruption/cleanup/invariant
        information. Receipt is causal evidence of accepted/started/published/rejected/
        interrupted/finalized work; pendingWork is test/tooling inventory, not actor
        state or settlement. Operational issue identity includes actor incarnation when
        present. Clearing an owner retains actor incarnation, source, and binding, but omits attempt
        generation. Later generation success or release by the same owner clears earlier
        operational occurrences. Later actor lifetimes cannot clear, inherit, or republish
        them. Invariant/cleanup issues may survive disposed snapshot; private
        stable diagnostics survive operational-summary clearing. Never treat receipt as
        actor state or pending inventory as settlement.
    O6  Stories, evidence, and capture
        Story construction and command grammar belong to @plan.PublicSurface.O5.
        Story evidence and capture belong to @plan.Artifacts.O3 and @plan.Artifacts.O7.
        Runtime owns the production execution path those stories exercise.

        Stories use typed RuntimeSetup, production Runtime, fixture Implementation,
        exact app refs, Story-local recipes, acknowledged dispatch, and Effect TestClock.

        - Materialize providers before consumers.
        - Expose handles through leases.
        - Dispose leases in reverse dependency order on every exit. App
        Stories use production RuntimeSetup/Runtime and do not inject context directly.
        Pure model exploration owns its candidate events and contains no Effect.run*,
        transaction commit, or stream subscription; fixtures may provide static seeds
        only. App Story cross-actor orchestration uses the real Runtime. Live proof is
        path.story.run(); do not confuse it with one predicted machine model. Story plans
        are inert until run(). Recipes are exact-machine/fresh-input/exact-binding
        descriptions with no runtime, mailbox, snapshot, state, operation binding, lease,
        or handle. App Stories target exact recipe or app-owned stable ref. Machine
        Stories own one implicit fresh actor, target-free commands, selected initial
        context, and setContext. Fixture is per-run environment. Commands are process,
        advance, advanceTo, advanceToNextTimer, checkpoint, run, and
        send. setContext appears only on machine Stories. process drains ready work without
        moving time. Clock movement does not process implicitly. run() is the sole execution
        boundary. Story plans
        inject no operation results; complete Implementations supply external behavior.
        Evidence captures exact actor lookup, runtime.now, runtime.pendingWork, and
        automatic run.end. Capture does not progress, move time, create/dispose/restore,
        or perform external work; run.end does not imply completion. Checkpoint is
        immediate capture. Reject bare app/live runtime Story input, focused
        boot/memory/state/snapshot overrides, extra actors, direct app-Story context
        injection, result injection, and package-private mechanisms.

        Evidence hub orders TurnRecord and LifecycleRecord before command acknowledgment.
        TurnPlan is pure: it applies one command to one actor snapshot and stabilizes
        redirects. It records intended state/memory, receipt intents, and activity changes.
        It makes no Effect or completed-Effect claims. Actions materialize finite
        plans only after guard acceptance; onMemory reconciles continuing plans separately.
        CommitPlan is package-private reconciliation commands. Both plans remain private.
        TurnRecord records actual committed facts; LifecycleRecord is private lifecycle
        evidence, not a machine turn. Runtime-global evidence is ordered before
        acknowledgment, sinks process asynchronously after ack and never reread mutable
        actor state or mutate runtime truth. Every successful turn exposes one atomic
        actor/store cut. Accepted evidence corresponds only to committed cuts. Lifecycle
        record includes full lifecycle snapshot, actor/app/plan provenance, from/to/cause,
        publication revision, machine-turn revision, timestamp, and evidence sequence.
        Allocation/locking/release choreography is private. Sinks are bounded and
        observational, use one global sequence for lifecycle and machine records, and
        may truncate only their retained prefix. No inline sink invocation, separate
        mutable histories, post-publication sequence exhaustion, overflow that blocks or
        rolls back publication, or synthetic terminal TurnRecord. Acknowledgment follows
        publication/evidence acceptance without awaiting user Effects; lifecycle snapshot
        precedes its event. Disposal drains accepted evidence after terminal lifecycle
        records. Permit/reservation/queue/gate choreography is private.

        A behaviorally visible phased remote lease belongs to an explicitly owned
        AppPlan actor. A purely operational continuing lease may be Effect.acquireRelease
        in a scoped stream activity. Normal completion/interruption/release failure are
        distinct and release occurs exactly once. No child-machine owner, new lifetime
        registry, hidden cancellation channel, or child-equivalent primitive.

        Public timestamps are integer epoch milliseconds from the Runtime Effect Clock;
        Stories/evidence use TestClock. Flow copies every envelope, record, array,
        canonical argument/key copy, reader, issue vector, and snapshot container.
        Application-owned opaque values retain identity. Checkpoint capture excludes
        unrelated actors, performs no live lookup from actor(...), releases read leases
        after `run.end` is captured, and captures `run.end` before cleanup. A Story
        checkpoint and successful `run.end` share one DehydrateBarrier cut of committed
        published state. The cut includes static Story closure, one StoreState revision,
        published actor snapshots, pending work, time, and accepted evidence prefix. Then
        release leases.

        Capture cannot perform these actions:

        - Mutate prior snapshots or look up a live actor.
        - Move time or create, restore, or dispose actors.
        - Perform external work or serialize selected context as a second truth source.
        FlowStoryExecutionError retains checkpoints/end evidence, failure boundary,
        ordered cleanup diagnostics, cancellation evidence, and sequence facts;
        declared Flow errors retain complete Cause. Actor snapshots do not expose Cause;
        capture and hydration follow @plan.Artifacts.O3.

        Boot is package-private RuntimeSetup data with Flow structural data and opaque
        application payloads. Persistence produces/consumes it; consumers cannot provide
        boot, decode, or hydrate. Flow validates structure/identity; applications validate
        opaque values through codec. Artifacts/CLI represent accepted app Stories, exact
        actor evidence lookup, run.end, module ownership, compound states, context
        requirements, lifecycle records, and operation identities. Public boot payloads,
        decoders, hydration calls, or artifacts omitting required identity/evidence are
        excluded. Artifact and CLI disposition follows @plan.Cutover.O2; host writes follow
        @plan.Hosts.O2.


        | Owner | Owns | Must not be duplicated by |
        | --- | --- | --- |
        | AppPlan | graph; IDs; requirements; ownership; reachability | runtime/adapter/Story/CLI |
        | RuntimeShell | boot; providers; scopes; readiness; cleanup | React/Story/host callback |
        | ActorEngine | mailbox, state, publish, cursors, activity reconcile | StoreKernel, React |
        | StoreKernel | StoreState; generations; overlays; revisions | actor cache/RcMap/adapter |
        | StoreFanout | revision delivery to actor mailbox | direct StoreState subscription |
        | owner lease | individual actor disposal | actor handle/ActorRef/React |
        | host adapter | managed Flow entry | sync runSync mutation |

decisions
    D1  One compiled graph owner
        AppPlan resolves the closed graph once for admission, operation ownership, inspection, 
        fixtures, and tooling. Runtime and hosts consume those indexes.
    D2  One runtime lifecycle owner
        RuntimeShell owns bootstrap, readiness, scopes, and cleanup. ActorEngine owns one actor 
        mailbox and publication; StoreKernel ownership is @plan.OperationsV2.O1.
    D3  Selected restore order
        Prepare and validate detached persistence data before acquiring Implementation;
        install prepared StoreKernel state, actors, and operations after acquisition.
        WIRE-004–006 provide source traceability. ARCH-008's earlier order is
        historical and superseded by this selected Workflow order; it creates no open
        behavior or source-reconciliation question.
constraints
    C1  Keep identity and executable input separate
        Use exact machine, actor, operation, Story, and recipe identity domains. Keep complete 
        executable P separate from canonical K; @plan.OperationsV2.O1 owns store consequences.
    C2  Limit Effect execution to host edges
        Only the Runtime bridge or final host adapter runs Effects. Internal workflows return 
        Effects and preserve typed failures, defects, and interruption.
questions
    Q1  Pinned Effect composition remains open
        Which Effect rc.112 composition satisfies readiness, actor, and cleanup ownership?
        Keep recommended service/layer/helper topology nonnormative. Verify APIs before
        implementation claims.
verification
    V1  Definitions and state scope
        Establish @O1 when these scenarios pass:

        - Compile exact state, event, input, context, and memory literals.
        - Reject invalid names at byte and control-character boundaries.
        - Accept state depth ten and reject depth eleven.
        - Initialize fresh actors once; restore and resume without replay.
        - Resolve defaults, redirects, and reentry to exact active leaves.
        - Prove recursive substates share one actor identity and lifetime.

        Candidate paths: `facade.compile.ts`, `actors.test.ts`, and `turns.test.ts` under
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        UTF-8, full Schema, and state-depth matrices remain unverified.
    V2  Closed graph and identity
        Establish @O2 when these scenarios pass:

        - Preserve exact machine keys when flattening ordered modules.
        - Reject duplicate machine IDs, machine values, and tooling owners.
        - Keep module identity separate from machine and durable actor identity.
        - Resolve descriptors per runtime and reject hostile P/K inputs before work.

        Candidate paths: `assembly.test.ts`, `admission.test.ts`, `facade.compile.ts`,
        and `structural-copy-owner.test.ts` under
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        Exact collision, cross-runtime, and canonical-key cases remain pending.
    V3  Construction and admission
        Establish @O3 when these scenarios pass:

        - Keep construction inert and cache one terminal readiness result.
        - Restore providers before consumers and validate exact context closure.
        - Join concurrent ensures and roll back invisible admission in reverse order.
        - Preserve cleanup truth after lease visibility and retain stable tombstones.
        - Dispose during pending acquisition without leaking partial graph state.
        - Preserve typed failure, defect, and interruption separately.

        Candidate tests live under `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`:

        - `runtime-shell-owner.test.ts`, `readiness.test.ts`, and `admission.test.ts`.
        - `actor-admission.test.ts`, `actor-lease-owner.test.ts`, and `context.test.ts`.
        Full persistence, codec, and restore coverage remains pending.
    V4  Turn publication and failure
        Establish @O4 when these scenarios pass:

        - Order commands, timers, StoreFanout facts, and context facts through one mailbox.
        - Discard invalid candidates without partial state, memory, store, or work changes.
        - Return from public send synchronously; acknowledge Stories after publication.
        - Replay the latest snapshot and complete streams after terminal publication.
        - Preserve typed failure, defect, and interruption while settling every acknowledgment.

        Candidate paths: `turns.test.ts`, `turn-plan-owner.test.ts`, `actors.test.ts`,
        `lifecycle.test.ts`, and `actor-evidence.test.ts` under
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        Atomic operation/store behavior remains owned by @plan.OperationsV2.O1.
    V5  Lifecycle and context
        Establish @O5 when these scenarios pass:

        - Propagate provider waves atomically; reject invalid bindings before side effects.
        - Preserve actor state and logical context edges across suspension.
        - Reject suspended commands and never replay input or finite work on resume.
        - Wait for finalizers before resuming; report dependent-disposal diagnostics.
        - Replace selector dependencies after evaluation and rerun on matching publications.
        - Keep passive reads free of actor creation, ownership, and work.

        Candidate tests live under `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`:

        - `context.test.ts`, `context-wave-owner.test.ts`, and `context-lineage-owner.test.ts`.
        - `lifecycle.test.ts`, `actor-lease-owner.test.ts`, `copies.test.ts`, and `actors.test.ts`.
        Host and React proofs remain pending.
    V6  Stories and evidence
        Establish @O6 when these scenarios pass:

        - Run live and Story commands through the same production owners.
        - Keep pure model exploration free of production Effects.
        - Reject reentrant host mutation and accept evidence before acknowledgment.
        - Drain accepted evidence during disposal and capture one committed checkpoint cut.
        - Perform no external work during capture; copy containers and retain opaque values.

        Candidate paths: `evidence.test.ts`, `actor-evidence.test.ts`,
        `evidence-journal-owner.test.ts`, and `copies.test.ts` under
        `packages/flow-state-rewrite/src/runtime-next/test/`.
        Story, host, artifact, persistence, and checkpoint integration proofs remain pending.
